export const FR_C2_S08_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "fr-c2-eloge-de-l-ennui": [
    `I would like to praise here a feeling that everything around us is bent on making disappear, and which has nonetheless given humanity most of its daydreams, some of its masterpieces and, I suspect, every last one of its ripples on a pond: boredom. I don't mean melancholy, which has its poets, nor depression, which has its doctors. I mean ordinary boredom, commonplace, without grandeur, the boredom of Sunday afternoons and waiting rooms, the one our grandparents knew by heart and that our children may never meet.`,
    `I had lost sight of it myself for a good fifteen years. Not that my life was thrilling; but there was always something in my pocket to fill it. A queue? My emails. A metro carriage? The news of the world, which I checked with the greed of a man afraid it would change without him. The slightest gap in the day was instantly plugged by a screen, the way you seal a crack before it lets the air in. I was no longer bored. I wasn't thinking much either, but that I hadn't noticed.`,
    `Two years ago, on a Tuesday in November, the Paris–Clermont train I was travelling on came to a halt in open country, somewhere between Nevers and Moulins, because of an "overhead line incident", as the set phrase goes, which nobody ever found out the nature of. It was two in the afternoon. My phone, which I had forgotten to charge, gave up the ghost at six minutes past two. The network, in any case, was non-existent. We were told the delay was "indefinite". I had, I admit, a moment of genuine panic, as if an organ had been removed.`,
    `The first hour was dreadful. I looked at my dead phone the way you look at the door of a friend who no longer answers. I read the safety notice three times, then the ingredients of a packet of biscuits. I was restless, sullen, vaguely humiliated. In the second hour, something gave way. I began to look out of the window, not to see anything, but for lack of anything better. There was a meadow, a hedge, three Charolais cows, a low, leaden-grey sky. Nothing was happening. And it was precisely because nothing was happening that my mind, deprived of food, began to make its own.`,
    `I thought again of my father, dead for five years, and of something he had said to me one day on a station platform that I hadn't understood. I understood it. I recalled the title of a book I had loved at sixteen. I had an idea, then another, then a third that contradicted the first two and was better. My neighbour, an elderly lady who had been knitting since Paris without looking up, finally said to me, still counting her stitches: "It took you a while, but you're there. Boredom is the waiting room of ideas. You have to agree to stay in it a little."`,
    `Pascal wrote that all of humanity's unhappiness comes from one single thing, which is not knowing how to stay quietly in a room. This sentence is often quoted to draw a lesson in wisdom from it; people forget that it is first of all an admission of failure. Pascal knew that we flee rest because it puts us face to face with ourselves, and that this encounter is rarely flattering. Diversion, in the sense he meant, is not a pleasure: it is an escape. Our screens have invented nothing. They have only made the escape so convenient that we no longer even notice we are running.`,
    `The train set off again at twenty past five. As soon as we approached Moulins, the network came back, and throughout the carriage you heard the same little electronic chime, dozens of times, like a flock of sheep getting its bells back. I saw my neighbours lean, in a single movement, over their screens. My knitter put away her work, smiled at me and got off. I felt, I must say, a pang that resembled regret: something had opened up during those three hours, and I could already feel it closing again.`,
    `Since then I have been trying to be bored. It's harder than it looks. Boredom can't be ordered; you have to leave room for it, as for a wild animal that only comes close if you make no movement. I sometimes leave my phone at home. I sit in a park without reading anything. And I have ended up, I confess, writing in my diary, every Thursday, from six to six-thirty: "Be bored." That is no doubt proof that I have understood nothing. But at least, for thirty minutes, I'm not running.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-la-lettre-de-madame-valade": [
    `Maître Bertrand Lascoux had practised for thirty years in Périgueux, in an office with dark panelling where family secrets were passed down with the land, and his principle was never to be surprised. Hélène Valade's will, however, had shaken him out of his reserve. The old lady, widow of a wine merchant, left the whole of her estate, a house in Brantôme, a few hectares of walnut trees and a very respectable share portfolio, to her housekeeper, Josiane, who had served her for twenty-two years. Her only son, Laurent, received only the reserved portion that the law forbids taking from him.`,
    `The will came with a sealed envelope in Laurent's name, accompanied by an instruction in the deceased's own hand: "To be given to my son only in the event that he contests my dispositions. If he accepts them, destroy it unopened." Maître Lascoux had put the envelope in his safe, between a deed of sale and a deed of gift, and had looked at it more often than he would have cared to admit.`,
    `Laurent Valade came to the office one Thursday in March. He was a man of about fifty, a maths teacher in Bordeaux, divorced, with the tired look of those who have waited too long for something that never came. He listened to the reading of the will without flinching. At the end he sat still for a moment, then said in a calm voice that he would not contest it. "My mother always knew what she wanted. Josiane was there. I wasn't. It's fair, no doubt." He signed what needed signing and left without shaking anyone's hand.`,
    `The notary had a bad night. He knew the instruction was clear, that it bound his conscience as much as his profession, and that he now had to destroy the envelope. He also knew he had just seen a man leave with, in his eyes, the certainty of not having been loved. What was in that letter? An explanation, an accusation, a final reproach? A declaration of love, perhaps, that the old lady had never managed to make in her lifetime? He had no right to open it. He had no right to hand it over. He had only the right to burn it, and that was the one act that seemed unbearable to him.`,
    `For three weeks he put it off until the next day. He consulted two colleagues, without naming names, one of whom advised him to respect the dead woman's wishes and the other to retire. He reread the code, where he found nothing about posthumous love letters. One evening he caught himself holding the envelope up against his desk lamp, and put it down with the shame of a schoolboy caught out. The law, he told himself, protects the living against the will of the dead; it doesn't protect the dead against the tenderness of the living.`,
    `It was Laurent who came back. He turned up without an appointment, one Friday evening, just as the secretary was closing the office. He looked even more tired. "I know there's a letter," he said. "Josiane told me. My mother had mentioned it to her. I don't want anything else. Not the house, not the walnut trees. I'd just like the letter." The notary, his throat tight, explained the condition. The letter could only be handed over in the event of a contestation. Laurent looked at him for a long time, then, for the first time, he smiled. "Then I contest."`,
    `Maître Lascoux, who had spent thirty years distrusting subtleties, silently admired this one. He himself drafted, on a corner of his desk, a contestation in due form, which Laurent signed and which they both knew would be withdrawn the next morning. Then he fetched the envelope from the safe and handed it to the son. Laurent opened it there and then, with very slow movements. It contained only one sheet, covered in his mother's tight, slanting handwriting. He read it, reread it, then handed it to the notary. "Read it. You've earned it."`,
    `"My Laurent, if you are reading this, it means you contested. I knew you would, and I knew it wouldn't be for the money, because you never wanted anything from me except what I didn't know how to give you. I left my estate to Josiane because she needs it and you never have. But I left you the only thing that mattered to me: the certainty that you would come and look for it. I never knew how to tell you. I loved you. I just needed you to insist." Maître Lascoux handed back the sheet, stood up, and went to stand at the window for a long time, for reasons that were his own business.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-trois-lettres": [
    `Nadia Benali, forty-four, a chartered accountant in Vitré, had received a registered letter in January informing her that she had been drawn by lot to sit as a juror at the assize court of Ille-et-Vilaine. She had first looked for grounds for exemption, found none, and presented herself one Monday morning at the Parliament of Brittany building in Rennes, with the vague apprehension of someone summoned to an exam without knowing the subject.`,
    `The case was the kind that divides family dinners. Thierry Le Bihan, fifty-two, a nurse, was accused of having killed his mother, who was suffering from a neurodegenerative disease at the terminal stage, by giving her a massive dose of morphine. He denied nothing. He explained that she had asked him to, dozens of times, as long as she could still speak, then by blinking when she no longer could. The prosecution was seeking a conviction for premeditated murder, while hinting, in a closing speech of remarkable caution, that the court would know how to weigh the sentence.`,
    `For four days Nadia listened. She heard the doctors, who spoke of palliative care and deep sedation, of what the law allowed and what it forbade, with distinctions so fine they seemed drawn with a scalpel. She heard the accused's sister, who blamed him for acting alone, without telling her, and who wept from anger more than grief. She heard a neighbour, a care assistant, a priest. She looked at the accused, who never lowered his eyes and who had the hands of a man used to caring for people.`,
    `On the last day, before the court withdrew, the presiding judge read aloud the instruction the law requires to be read to jurors, which is posted in large letters in the deliberation room. Nadia already knew it; they had been shown it the first morning. Yet she heard it for the first time. The law, this text said in substance, does not ask judges to account for the means by which they have been convinced. It asks them only to question themselves, in silence and contemplation, and to seek, in the sincerity of their conscience, what impression the evidence has made on their reason. One single question: do you have an inner conviction?`,
    `The deliberation lasted seven hours. The three professional judges and the six jurors sat around a large oval table, beneath the poster. There was a lot of talk. A retired railway worker asserted that the law was the law and that you couldn't let everyone decide when their mother should die. A young history student replied, her voice trembling, that she would have done the same. A craftsman from Fougères said nothing for three hours, then simply said that he had sat up with his dying father and that he didn't know. Nadia said nothing. She was an accountant: she was used to columns that have to balance. This one didn't balance.`,
    `For the question put to her was not the one she would have liked to be asked. She was not being asked whether Thierry Le Bihan was a good man, or whether he had loved his mother, or whether the end-of-life law was just. She was being asked whether he was guilty of deliberately causing death. And the answer to that question she knew, in the sense that one knows a fact. What she didn't know was whether the word "guilty" could hold everything she had heard at once. She reflected that perhaps there is no word for that, and that this was precisely why juries had been invented.`,
    `The ballots were handed out. Each person had to write, alone, shielded by a hand or a folded arm, a yes or a no, then fold it and put it in the urn. Seven votes out of nine were needed to convict. Nadia took the pen. She thought of her own mother, who lived in Saint-Malo and phoned her every Sunday at noon precisely. She thought of the blinking. She thought of the sister, her anger, the fact that you can love someone and still steal a farewell from them. She thought that the sincerity of her conscience resembled, that evening, a room without windows.`,
    `She wrote three letters, folded the paper in four and slipped it into the urn. Later, when she was asked, like all the jurors, never to reveal her vote, she had no trouble promising. Driving back to Vitré in the night, she realized that she herself no longer quite knew what she had written, or rather that she knew she had written both. The following Sunday, at noon precisely, the phone rang. She let it ring three times, for nothing, for the pleasure of hearing that it still rang, and she picked up.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-dialogue-sur-le-pardon": [
    `Ginette Faure is ninety-seven, with a stick she uses only to point at things and a memory that historians from Limoges come to consult the way one consults a source. In June 1944, at seventeen, she carried messages for the maquis, hidden in the handlebars of her bicycle. Her brother Henri, two years older, was arrested on 9 June in a barn above the village, after being denounced, and shot the next day against the cemetery wall. She never knew who had denounced him. Until the Tuesday in October when a young man knocked at her door.`,
    `His name was Mathis Delmas, thirty-two, an engineer in Toulouse. While clearing out his grandfather's house after his death in the spring, he had found, in a shoebox, the draft of a letter addressed to the Kommandantur in Tulle, dated 8 June 1944, and signed. He laid it on the kitchen table, between the oilcloth and the coffee pot, without quite letting go of it. "I've come to ask your forgiveness, madame. For him. He never had the courage to do it."`,
    `Ginette looked at the yellowed paper for a long time without touching it. "Do you know what you're asking me, young man?" "I think so." "I don't think so. You're asking me to forgive, in my brother's name, a man who is dead, for something he didn't do to me. I can't forgive in Henri's place. He's the only one who would have had the right, and that right was taken from him along with everything else. And you can't ask forgiveness in your grandfather's place. These things can't be delegated. Neither the offence nor the forgiveness."`,
    `Mathis flushed. "So what do I do? Go away with this? Burn it? Do as he did, keep quiet for eighty years?" Ginette gave a short, dry laugh that was not unkind. "You see, you're starting to ask the right questions. Forgiveness I have none to give you, since you've done nothing to me. You were barely an idea in your great-grandmother's head. But there is something else you can do, which he didn't."`,
    `She stood up, slowly, and took two odd cups from the dresser. She poured the coffee without asking if he wanted any. "Do you know what I thought for eighty years? That maybe it was the mayor. Or the baker, who'd fallen out with my father. Or the neighbour, who was a bit too friendly with the Germans. I suspected half the village. Some died without my ever speaking to them again, because of that suspicion. Your grandfather didn't only take my brother from me. He took the others from me, all the others, for eighty years."`,
    `Mathis lowered his head. "What I'm asking of you," she went on, "isn't an apology. It's the truth. That paper must be deposited in the departmental archives, with his name, with yours if you have the courage, and with what you know about him. Not just that. Everything else too. Whether he was a good father, whether he was afraid, whether he had debts, whether the Germans had a hold on him. You never understand an act of cowardice unless you know the whole man. And you never give the others back their innocence unless you name the guilty one."`,
    `They talked until evening. He described the grandfather he had known, a gentle, silent man who mended the neighbourhood children's bikes and sometimes cried at the 8 May ceremonies without anyone knowing why. She described Henri, who wanted to be a teacher, who sang out of tune and who had taught her to ride a bicycle with no hands. At one point, without noticing, they started laughing together over a story about a runaway cow. Then they fell silent, a little embarrassed, as if someone had caught them.`,
    `Before he left, Ginette walked him to the gate, leaning on his arm rather than on her stick. "I forgive you nothing," she said, "since there's nothing to forgive you. But come back. Not for him. For me. I'm ninety-seven and I've just got half a village back. I'd like to make the most of it." The document was deposited in the Corrèze archives the following December. In the file there are two names at the bottom of the deposit record: that of Mathis Delmas and, just below, in a shaky but very legible hand, that of Ginette Faure.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-le-souper-de-madame-d-aubrac": [
    `On Tuesdays there was supper at the Marquise d'Aubrac's, rue Saint-Dominique, and people came less for the fare, which was decent, than for the conversation, which was not always so. The marquise had the art of bringing together at one table people who would never have spoken to one another elsewhere, and of making them believe they had chosen each other. That February evening in 1772, there was Abbé Morel, canon of Notre-Dame, greedy and subtle; Doctor Lassagne, physician to half the faubourg, a taciturn man reputed to know more secrets than all the confessors in Paris; and Monsieur Duverger, philosopher, tireless contributor to the Encyclopédie, who had never met a certainty without calling it into question, except his own.`,
    `It was the marquise who launched the subject, between the soup and the roast, the way one throws a ball to well-trained dogs. "Doctor," she said, "you who see so many people die: should a man be told he is going to die?" Doctor Lassagne put down his fork, looked at his fellow guests one by one, and answered that he preferred, this time, to listen. He added only that the question arose for him every week, and that he had never found the same answer twice.`,
    `Duverger did not wait to be asked. The truth, he said, was owed to every man, and above all to the dying man, because he was the only one to whom it truly belonged. To hide his end from him was to treat him as a child, to rob him of the last hours in which he could still settle his affairs, see again those he loved, make peace with his enemies or curse them with full knowledge. "The charitable lie, madame, is the most insolent of all. It assumes one knows better than the other what the other can bear."`,
    `Abbé Morel, who was savouring a Burgundy with almost mystical attention, agreed, which raised more than one eyebrow. "For once, the philosopher and I are in agreement, though for opposite reasons. The dying must be told the truth, not so that they can settle their affairs, but so that they can settle their souls. A man who dies without knowing it dies without confession, and I know no greater cruelty than to send him before his Judge without his having been able to prepare his defence." Duverger observed that, in his system, the Judge in question did not exist; the abbé replied that, in his, this changed nothing about the urgency.`,
    `The marquise took the side of the lie, with a grace that made it almost respectable. She had seen her father die, she said, who had been told of his condition with thoroughly philosophical frankness. He had spent his last three weeks trembling, counting the hours and cursing the doctors. "You speak of truth, gentlemen, as of a remedy. It is one, sometimes. But there are remedies that kill more surely than the disease. For my part, I should like to be allowed to believe to the very end that I have one spring left. I'll undertake to guess the rest."`,
    `The discussion went on until dessert, brilliant, lively, sometimes spiteful. Duverger quoted Montaigne and Epicurus, the abbé Saint Augustine, the marquise a provincial aunt who had said the same things in fewer words. Doctor Lassagne still had said nothing. He was watching Duverger with a peculiar attention, which the latter, carried away by his eloquence, did not notice. The marquise, who noticed everything, noticed it, and her hand tightened for a moment on her fan.`,
    `At the end of supper, as they moved to the drawing room, the doctor took the philosopher by the arm and led him into a window recess. He spoke in a low voice. He reminded Duverger of the pains he had been feeling in his side since the autumn, of the cough he said was of no importance, of the examination he had undergone the previous week, at his own request, whose conclusions the doctor had not yet told him. "You defended the truth very forcefully tonight, monsieur. I listened to you with the greatest attention. So you want it, this truth?"`,
    `Duverger looked for a long time at the rue Saint-Dominique, where the lanterns of carriages crossed in the snow. Then he turned round. He was very pale. "Tomorrow, doctor," he said. "Tell me tomorrow." The doctor bowed. Behind them, the Marquise d'Aubrac had come up without a sound. She said nothing, neither of lies nor of truth. She simply took the philosopher's arm, as one does with an old acquaintance, and led him towards the fire, where the abbé was telling a story about a bishop that made everyone laugh, including, to general surprise, Monsieur Duverger.`,
  ],
};

